# Problem Definition

To analyze and visualize patterns in road traffic accidents across the United States, using various factors such as weather conditions, severity (measured by length of traffic delay), locations, times, and contributing factors (e.g., speeding, distracted driving). The goal is to provide insights into the factors influencing the frequency and severity of accidents, identify high-risk locations and times, and explore accident types and their contributing causes.

# Research Objectives

What counties have the most accidents per capita and overall within the US

How do weather conditions influence accident frequency and severity?

What geographical locations are more likely to have higher accident rates? Cities or states

How frequent are accidents by the hour?

What are the most common accident types and their contributing factors?

# Importance of the Topic

Improvements for pedestrian and driver safety 

Update vehicle laws/policies

Infrastructure improvements 

Risk Reduction

# Population

## US Study Populations

-   This study will represent 48 of 50 of the states excluding Alaska and Hawaii

-   This data set includes over 7.7 million accident reports in the US

-   However, to ensure a more realistic data set, we will look at around 12 thousand points

### Content of Data

-   Data was collected in real-time using multiple traffic APIs.

-   Data was collected from February 2016 to March 2023.\

### Data Collection

-   Data was collected from the National and State level Departments of Transportation (DOT)

-   It also includes law enforcement agencies, traffic cameras, and traffic sensors within the road networks.

# About the Data

**Population**

-   Age, sex, education, & experience

**Traffic Conditions**

-   Location of accidents, severity, & street conditions

**Weather**

-   Weather conditions, time of day, temperature, etc.

Location

-   Maps, counties, cities, ZIP etc.

# **RO1: What counties have the most accidents per-capita and overall. Compare these counties across multiple characteristics, age, income, etc. using US census Data.**

## Dependent Variable

-   Accidents Per Capita

-   I am analyzing this against various Independent Variables \## Dependent Variables

-   Commuting Behavior

    -   Walk
    -   Drive
    -   Carpool
    -   Transit
    -   Work at Home
    -   Mean Commute

-   Employment Type

    -   Professional
    -   Service
    -   Office

## Operationalization of the Scale

-   **Dependent Variables**
-   Accidents Per Capita
    -   Operationalization
        -   Total Accidents per county/ Total Population of County
    -   Scale Type
        -   Ratio
-   **Independent Variables**

| Variable     | Operationalization                | Scale Type |
|--------------|-----------------------------------|------------|
| Walk         | \% of workers who walk to work    | Ratio      |
| Drive        | \% who drive alone                | Ratio      |
| Carpool      | \% who carpool                    | Ratio      |
| Transit      | \% who use public transit         | Ratio      |
| WorkAtHome   | \% who work remotely              | Ratio      |
| MeanCommute  | Average commute time in minutes   | Ratio      |
| Professional | %employed in professional roles   | Ratio      |
| Service      | \% employed in service jobs       | Ratio      |
| Office       | \% employed in office/admin roles | Ratio      |
|              |                                   |            |

## Data Wrangling

-   Refer to: Refernce data, of bigger data sets
    -   US Traffic Accident
        -   Data had 7 million rows so I wrangled the data and only kept sums and tables I needed
    -   US Census Data
        -   Originally had 100,000 rows and wrangled it down to 14,000 rows which is all i needed based on the counties i found in the US traffic Accident data

```{r}
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(readr)
library(scales)
library(patchwork)
#install.packages("tidytext")
library(tidytext)

#Read the big dataset with 7 million rows
US_Accident_Data <- read_csv("US_Accidents_March23.csv")

#Read Census data
US_Census_Data <- read_csv("2017 Census Data.csv")



# Summarize and arrange
top_counties <- US_Accident_Data %>%
  count(County, sort = TRUE) %>%
  top_n(20, n)

# Plot
ggplot(top_counties, aes(x = reorder(County, n), y = n)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
 coord_flip() +
  labs(title = "Top 20 Counties by Accident Count", x = "County", y = "Count")

top_counties_df <- top_counties %>%
  rename(Accident_Count = n)

print(top_counties_df)

write.csv(top_counties_df, "top_20_counties.csv", row.names = FALSE)

Top_20_Counties <- read_csv("top_20_counties.csv")

print(Top_20_Counties)


```

